# Loop Analysis

If you use an automated insulin delivery system (Loop, AAPS, Trio, iAPS or
OpenAPS), it makes small changes to your insulin all day long. **Loop Analysis**
shows you what those changes were, when they happened, and what followed. It
answers questions like _"does my loop always add insulin at 3 in the morning?"_,
_"what does my glucose do after breakfast?"_ and _"how often does the loop stop
running?"_

You'll find it under **Reports > Loop Analysis**. Only members of your account
can open it; it is not available on a public share link.

<Callout type="warning" title="Observations, not advice">

  Everything in Loop Analysis is an observation of what happened in your own
  data, with the number of days or events behind it. None of it is an
  instruction. Talk to your diabetes care team before you change any setting.

</Callout>

## The filter bar

Every tab shares the filter bar at the top, so you can switch tabs without
losing your choices.

<!-- Screenshot pending, declared in the screenshots manifest: loop-analysis-filter-bar -->

- **Range**: the dates being analysed, up to 90 days. A longer range is
  shortened to 90 days, and the bar says so.
- **Compare with**: a second period to draw alongside the first, either the
  period just before it or dates you pick. The comparison is drawn as dashed
  outlines over each chart, and each figure shows the compared value beside it.
- **Profile**: limit the analysis to the time a particular profile was active.
- **Days**: leave out days of the week, for example to look at weekdays only.
- **Exclude**: leave out the time you spent in an override, a temporary target,
  exercise, illness, or with the loop open (not running automatically). Illness
  and open loop are left out by default. Only that time is removed, not the
  whole day it fell on.

Times that your data marks as unreliable, such as compression lows (a false low
when you lie on your sensor) and sensor errors, are always left out of glucose.

## The tabs

| Tab | The question it answers |
|-----|-------------------------|
| **Daily profile** | What does a typical day look like, hour by hour? Where does the loop add or take away insulin? |
| **Meals & events** | What happens after a meal, a correction, a rise you didn't log, or the start of exercise or a new sensor? |
| **Predictions** | How close do the loop's predictions come to what actually happened? |
| **Loop health** | How much of the time was the loop running, and how often did it hit its limits? |
| **Settings evidence** | What do your own data suggest about your basal rates, correction factor and carb ratio? |

## Net loop correction

The headline figure on several tabs is **net loop correction**: the extra
insulin your loop gave, or held back, on top of your scheduled basal. It adds up
two things the algorithm decides:

- **Temp basals the algorithm set**, measured against your scheduled basal. A
  temp above schedule adds insulin; a temp below it (including a zero temp)
  takes insulin away.
- **Automatic boluses** (SMBs in AAPS, Trio and OpenAPS, or Loop's automatic
  boluses).

Automatic boluses are included because many systems do most of their correcting
with them. Leaving them out, as older tools did, would show only part of what
the loop does.

Some things are deliberately kept **out** of net loop correction and shown on
their own, because they are not the algorithm's decisions:

- temp basals you set by hand;
- pump suspends;
- boluses you gave yourself.

Trio, AAPS and OpenAPS don't label whether a temp basal came from the loop or
from you. Nocturne checks each temp against the decisions your loop uploaded
around it. A temp the loop asked for counts as the algorithm's. A temp the loop's
decisions show it didn't ask for counts as manual. A temp with no loop decisions
uploaded near it stays counted as the algorithm's, since that's what these
systems almost always do.

## Reading the charts

Most charts in Loop Analysis show a shaded band rather than a single line.

- The **line** in the middle is the median: half your days (or readings) were
  above it and half below.
- The **darker band** covers the middle half (25th to 75th percentile).
- The **lighter band** covers most of your data (10th to 90th percentile).
- **n** is how many days, readings or events a figure is based on.

Two kinds of figure are measured slightly differently:

- **Glucose** pools every reading from every day into each five-minute slot,
  the same way the AGP report does, so the bands agree with AGP. Its n counts
  readings.
- **Rates** (basal, net loop correction, insulin on board and carbs on board)
  are worked out for each day first and then compared across days. Their n
  counts days. A day only counts in a slot when at least half of that slot has
  data.

A figure based on **fewer than 5 days** is drawn faded and marked as sparse:
there isn't enough behind it to read it as a pattern.

Missing data is shown as missing, never as zero. A zero temp is a real zero and
always counts.

### When parts are hidden

If someone has shared your data with limited access, some parts of the report
may say **"Hidden: your access does not include this data"**. Each part needs
access to the kind of data it is drawn from: glucose, treatments (insulin and
carbs), devices (what the loop and pump recorded) or therapy settings (your
profile). Filters you can't use are greyed out, with the access they need.

Someone who can see only the **last 24 hours** sees that, whatever dates they
pick, with no comparison. The report tells them so.

## Daily profile

<!-- Screenshot pending, declared in the screenshots manifest: loop-analysis-daily-profile -->

The Daily profile lays your days on top of each other, in five-minute slots of
the local day, so patterns by time of day stand out. From top to bottom:

- **Glucose**, with your target range behind it.
- **Basal**: what your schedule asked for and what was actually delivered.
- **Net loop correction**: what the loop added or took away, with its spread.
  The hourly chart underneath sums it by hour.
- **Insulin on board** and **carbs on board**.
- **Manual boluses** and **carbs**, showing how often and how much at each time
  of day.

The tiles across the top give the totals: insulin per day, the basal and bolus
split, automatic against manual boluses, delivered against scheduled basal, and
net loop correction per day. Daily totals use only days with at least 90% of the
day covered by delivery records, and are never scaled up from partial days.

Click a slot, or press Enter on it, to list the days behind it. Each day links
to **Day in Review** at that time.

## Meals & events

<!-- Screenshot pending, declared in the screenshots manifest: loop-analysis-meals-events -->

This tab lines up every event of a kind at the moment it happened, then shows
what followed: the change in glucose, glucose itself, the loop's correction,
insulin above your schedule since the event, and insulin and carbs on board.
You can compare up to three kinds of event at once.

### The events you can pick

- **Meals**: a carb entry. Entries at the same moment count as one meal.
- **Correction boluses**: a bolus you gave with no carbs logged within 30
  minutes either side.
- **Unannounced rises**: a rise in glucose that looks like a meal but has no
  carbs logged. See below.
- **Sensor starts**, and the start of **exercise**, an **override** or a
  **temporary target**.

You choose how long before and after each event to show (by default an hour
before and five hours after), and can limit an event kind to a time of day or,
for meals, a range of grams.

### How an unannounced rise is found

Nocturne looks for a pattern that a meal would make:

1. **A stable floor.** Glucose holds steady for at least 30 minutes, moving no
   more than 20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L), at 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L) or above.
2. **A climb.** From there, glucose rises at least 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L) within
   45 minutes without dropping back.
3. **Not a sensor artefact.** A climb faster than 5 mg/dL (0.3 mmol/L) a minute,
   or a single jump of 30 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L) or more, is set aside as noise. So
   is the shape of a **compression recovery**: a sharp drop onto the floor
   followed by a climb straight back to where glucose was, which is what
   lying on your sensor looks like.
4. **Not logged.** A rise with carbs logged in the three hours before it, or
   with carbs still on board, isn't unannounced.

Optionally, you can ask for **only rises the loop answered**: rises where the
loop added at least half an hour of your usual basal (and at least 0.2 U) in the
45 minutes after the rise began. This filters out rises your loop didn't treat
as real.

### Rises and falls

Each kind of event has an expected direction. After a meal or an unannounced
rise, glucose usually increases. After a correction bolus, the start of exercise,
an override or a temporary target, glucose usually decreases. A sensor start
uses the figures for a rise.

For a rise, the table shows the **peak rise**, the **time to peak** and the
time until glucose is near the start glucose again.

For a fall, the table shows these figures:

- **Drop to window end**: the start glucose minus the glucose at the end of the
  window. Settings evidence uses the same method, over 4 hours. The lowest
  reading is not used for the drop, because noise makes it too low.
- **Lowest glucose** and the **time to lowest glucose**.
- **Drop per unit above schedule**: the drop divided by the net insulin above
  your schedule in the window. This figure is only for isolated events with at
  least 0.5 U above schedule that did not go below range.
- **Went below range**: how many events had a reading below 70 mg/dL in the
  window.

### Isolated events

An event is **isolated** when no other meal, correction or meal-like rise
happens in its window. Tick **Isolated events only** to keep just those. Without
it, the curves after a meal may also include the next snack.

The **observed grams per unit** (how many grams of carbs each unit of insulin
covered) is shown only for isolated meals. For a meal that overlaps another, the
insulin in the window wasn't for that meal alone, so dividing its grams by it
would mislead. It also needs delivery records for at least 90% of the window.

## Predictions

<!-- Screenshot pending, declared in the screenshots manifest: loop-analysis-predictions -->

Your loop predicts where your glucose is heading, and decides what to do based
on that. This tab checks those predictions against the readings that actually
followed, at 30, 60, 90 and 120 minutes ahead.

- **Bias** is the average of _predicted minus actual_. A positive bias means
  the predictions ran high; a negative one, that they ran low.
- **MAE** (mean absolute error) is how far off the predictions were on average,
  whichever direction.
- **n** is how many predictions were checked.

A prediction is only paired with a reading within 2.5 minutes of the time it
predicted; with none that close, it's counted as unmatched rather than guessed.

### Why only 30 and 60 minutes matter for settings

A prediction assumes nothing else will happen. But the loop keeps acting after
it makes a prediction: by two hours later, glucose reflects all of that too. So
only the **30 and 60 minute bias** says something about your settings. A
consistent bias there, for example predictions that always run high after
meals, is worth raising with your care team. The longer horizons are shown for
interest.

### Interrupted predictions

If you ate or gave a bolus after a prediction was made but before the time it
predicted, the prediction couldn't have known about it. Those horizons are
counted as **interrupted** and left out of every figure.

### Curves and contexts

Loop makes one prediction. AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS make several, each
assuming something different: **IOB** (insulin only), **zero temp** (if basal
stopped), **COB** (carbs absorbing as entered) and **UAM** (unannounced meal,
from the rise in glucose). Each curve is checked in the situations the algorithm
uses it in, such as after a meal, after a correction, or fasting.

Two curves come with a built-in bias in which predictions get checked:

- **UAM** is only made while glucose is rising faster than insulin alone
  explains, so its accuracy is only measured during rises.
- **Zero temp** is cut short where it turns back up above target, so its long
  horizons come only from predictions that stayed low.

If your access doesn't include treatments, the curves that are read only after
meals or corrections are hidden, and the tab names them.

## Loop health

<!-- Screenshot pending, declared in the screenshots manifest: loop-analysis-loop-health -->

Loop health shows how steadily your loop ran. Each tile is a share of the time
being analysed, and the charts break it down by hour of the day.

| Tile | What it means |
|------|---------------|
| **Closed loop** | Time the loop was in control. |
| **CGM active** | Time your sensor was sending readings. |
| **Pump active** | Time the loop was talking to the pump. |
| **Loop gaps** | Stretches of more than 15 minutes between two loop cycles. |
| **Unrecorded delivery** | Time the loop was running but the pump's records don't say what it delivered. |
| **Algorithm zero temps** | Time the loop set basal to zero, as a share of the time delivery is known. Suspends and manual zero temps are shown separately. |
| **At max basal** | Time the loop's temp basals were at the highest rate it was allowed. |
| **At max IOB** | Time the loop held back because insulin on board had reached its limit (AAPS, Trio, OpenAPS). |
| **Automatic boluses a day** | How many, and how big. |
| **Suggested, not enacted** | Changes the loop asked for that weren't carried out, for example because the pump didn't respond. |
| **Max basal setting** and **Max IOB** | The limits your loop reported. |

### At max basal and "cap known"

For **Loop**, a temp is at its cap when it reaches your maximum basal rate
setting. For **AAPS, Trio and OpenAPS**, the cap is a safety limit the
algorithm works out each time, and only the loop's own explanation says when it
was reached. **Cap known** is the share of the algorithm's temp time where
Nocturne could tell either way; **at max basal** is measured over that time.

### Unrecorded delivery

When the loop is running but there is no record of what the pump delivered, the
basal for that time is unknown. Loop Analysis never assumes your schedule ran
for longer than three hours without a record. A lot of unrecorded delivery
usually means pump history isn't reaching Nocturne, and it makes the insulin
figures on the other tabs less complete.

### When a figure says "Unknown"

A tile that can't be worked out says why:

- **No loop cycles in this range**: nothing from the loop was recorded.
- **The system that ran the loop does not record this**: for example, Loop
  doesn't report a maximum IOB, and only Loop uploads a maximum basal rate
  setting.
- **No loop cycle recorded what this needs**: the loop ran, but didn't record the
  explanation the figure is read from.
- **No loop cycle in this range set the temps this needs**: no loop decision
  could be matched to the temps.
- **Everything this is measured on falls in time the filters leave out.**
- **The profile in force records no maximum basal rate.**
- **The loop set no temp basal in this range.**

## Settings evidence

Settings evidence puts what your data shows next to what your profile says, for
three settings:

- **Basal** in two-hour blocks of the day;
- **Correction factor (ISF)** and **carb ratio** in four blocks: overnight
  (midnight to 6am), morning (6 to 11), midday (11 to 5pm) and evening (5pm to
  midnight).

Each figure comes with how many days or events it is based on, how much they
varied, a confidence level, and the list of every day or event behind it.

<Callout type="warning" title="Discuss changes with your care team">

  These are observations of your own data, never recommendations. A figure can
  look convincing and still be wrong because of something the data can't see.
  Take them to your care team rather than changing settings on your own.

</Callout>

### How each figure is worked out

**Basal.** For each two-hour block on each day, Nocturne asks: what basal rate
would have kept glucose level? It starts from your scheduled rate, adds what the
loop added or took away, allows for insulin from earlier still acting (and for
insulin given in the block that acts later), and adjusts for any drift in
glucose across the block using your correction factor. The figure is the average
across the days that qualify. It's only shown when at least 70% of those days
agree on whether the loop was adding or taking away insulin, and not when
glucose was rising or falling steeply on average (more than 10 mg/dL, or
0.6 mmol/L, an hour).

**Insulin is counted when it was delivered, not when it acted.** The blocks are
two hours long because the loop's insulin acts over the next hour or two, and
the allowance for insulin still acting keeps the end of the evening's insulin
from looking like overnight need.

**Correction factor.** Each correction bolus is followed for four hours (three
if you eat after that). The figure is how far glucose fell, divided by the
insulin that acted, and the median across corrections is shown. Nocturne uses
the reading at the end of the window rather than the lowest point, because the
lowest of many readings tends to be a little too low.

**Carb ratio.** Each meal is followed from 15 minutes before it (to include a
pre-bolus) to four hours after. The figure is the grams eaten divided by the
insulin needed: the insulin that acted, adjusted for where glucose ended up
compared with where it started. The median across meals is shown.

The correction factor and carb ratio measure insulin above your **observed**
basal, not above your schedule. If your scheduled basal is too low, the loop
tops it up, and that extra insulin shouldn't be credited to the meal or
correction.

### What is left out

A day or event is left out if any of these applies anywhere in its window, and
the tab counts how many were left out for each reason.

**Basal blocks are left out when:**

- the loop was open, or an override, temporary target, exercise or illness was
  active;
- carbs were logged in the four hours before or during the block, or carbs were
  still on board;
- glucose was climbing in a way that looks like an unlogged meal;
- a bolus was given by hand in the five hours before or during the block;
- a temp was set by hand or the pump was suspended;
- delivery records cover less than 90% of the block, or the schedule had to be
  assumed;
- insulin on board isn't known at both ends of the block;
- there's no reading near the start or end of the block;
- the block falls outside your filters.

**Corrections are left out when:**

- carbs were logged in the hour before or the three hours after;
- another meal, correction or meal-like rise falls in the window;
- an override, temporary target, exercise or illness was active;
- glucose wasn't above your target range at the time, or moved more than
  15 mg/dL (0.8 mmol/L) in the 15 minutes before;
- insulin already on board was more than the bolus itself;
- glucose went below 70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L) in the window;
- less than 0.5 U of insulin acted;
- readings, delivery records or insulin on board are missing.

**Meals are left out when:**

- another meal, correction or meal-like rise falls in the window;
- an override, temporary target, exercise or illness was active;
- glucose at the start was below 70 or above 180 mg/dL (3.9 or 10.0 mmol/L), or
  moved more than 15 mg/dL (0.8 mmol/L) in the 15 minutes before the meal;
- less than 0.5 U of insulin was needed;
- readings, delivery records or insulin on board are missing.

A figure needs at least 5 days (basal) or 3 events (correction factor and carb
ratio) to be shown at all.

### Confidence

Each figure is rated **High**, **Moderate** or **Low**. It starts at High and
drops one level for each concern. Fewer than 6 days or events is always Low.
The concerns are:

- **Few episodes**: fewer than 10.
- **Wide spread**: the middle half of the values spans more than half the figure.
- **Borrowed correction factor**: glucose drift was converted using a correction
  factor that wasn't itself measured with confidence, and that conversion made
  up a real part of the figure.
- **Borrowed basal**: insulin was measured against a basal that wasn't measured
  with confidence.
- **Insulin still on board**: a lot of the insulin was still acting when the
  window ended.
- **Most events weren't isolated**: more than half were left out because
  something else happened in their window.
- **Fasting can't be checked**: carbs were logged on fewer than one day in five,
  so Nocturne can't tell fasting time from unlogged meals.
- **Still moving at the end**: glucose was still rising or falling at the end of
  the window.
- **Insulin from earlier mattered**: insulin still acting from before the block
  made up a real part of the basal figure.

### Why "more insulin" figures are held to a higher bar

When a figure would mean **more insulin** than your profile (a higher basal, a
lower correction factor, or a lower carb ratio) by 10% or more, and it leans on
another estimate, its confidence drops a further level. Getting it wrong in that
direction carries more risk of a low, so Nocturne asks for stronger evidence
before rating it highly.

## How it differs from Nightscout's Loopalyzer

Loop Analysis replaces Nightscout's Loopalyzer report and fixes the problems
that made it hard to trust:

- **Zeros count.** Loopalyzer skipped zeros when it averaged, so a zero temp
  made the loop look as if it had done more than it did.
- **Automatic boluses count.** Loopalyzer charted temp basals only, so users
  whose loop corrects with SMBs saw only a fraction of what it did.
- **Daylight saving time works.** Loopalyzer dropped days that weren't exactly
  24 hours long, and showed glucose an hour off after a change.
- **Readings exactly on a five-minute mark are kept**, and one missing basal
  record no longer shifts every later slot.
- **Spread and n are shown**, not just an average.
- **Totals**: insulin per day, split into basal, automatic and manual boluses.
- **Up to 90 days**, not 14.
- **Meals line up properly.** Loopalyzer's time shift used the first meal in the
  data rather than in the day, and lost data pushed past midnight.
- **You can leave things out**: overrides, temporary targets, exercise, illness
  and open-loop time.
- **It works the same way for Loop, AAPS, Trio, iAPS and OpenAPS.**
